.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH J0 3M
.SH NAME
j0, j1, jn, y0, y1, yn, erf, erfc \- bessel and error functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double j0(double x);
.PP
.B double j1(double x);
.PP
.B double jn(int n, double x);
.PP
.B double y0(double x);
.PP
.B double y1(double x);
.PP
.B double yn(int n, double x);
.PP
.B double erf(double x);
.PP
.B double erfc(double x);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions calculate Bessel functions of the first
and second kinds for real arguments and integer orders.
.PP
.B Note:
.I erf
and
.I erfc
are documented here because they have no page of their own and the two families
share the same provenance and the same fits.
.I Erf
returns the error function of
.IR x ;
.I erfc
returns 1 \- erf(x), computed directly rather than by that subtraction \- for
large
.I x
every significant digit of the result lies in the digits that would cancel.
Both are v7 extensions and not C11.  v7's <math.h> declared them and this tree's
silently did not, which was an oversight: the sources were always there.
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3), floor(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Non-positive arguments cause
.I y0, y1,
and
.I yn
to return \-HUGE_VAL
and set
.I errno
to EDOM.
.B Note:
v7 said "negative"; zero is a domain error too, and the value returned is the
most negative finite value this machine has, not a \-infinity it has not.
.PP
.I J0, j1, jn, erf
and
.I erfc
are finite everywhere and have no error return.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The leading coefficients did not fit, so they are scaled
This is the whole of the port for these two files.  v7's rational fits for
.I j0
and
.I j1
open with coefficients near 5e20 to 5e23, and the largest finite value on this
machine is 9.223e18 \-
.B so they cannot be written as literals at all.
.PP
Each numerator/denominator
.I pair
is divided by a power of ten (10**10, 10**11 or 10**13, noted on each array's
first line), which is a pure shift of every literal's decimal exponent and leaves
the ratio n/d \- the only thing either polynomial contributes \- unchanged.  The
pairs that already fit are v7's outright.
.PP
The Horner loops needed no unrolling:
.I b6cc
emits an initialized file-scope
.B double
array to the constant pool.
.SS gamma is absent
v7's
.I gamma
is the one routine of its libm whose source never reached this tree.  Nothing
here calls it, it is not C11, and <tgmath.h> does not name it either.  It comes
back with the first program that wants it, and the choice between v7's
log-gamma-plus-signgam and C11's tgamma/lgamma gets made then.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/j0.c, j1.c, jn.c, erf.c
